WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.220
Two fine nerve branches meet.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.780
Do they touch, or do they fuse?

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.680
A stain that marks every cell body shows a crowd of round cell bodies.

4
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.360
The thin branches stay almost invisible.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.220
Golgi's black reaction fills a few neurons completely, and most cells stay pale.

6
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.820
So a single branch can be followed within the section.

7
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.120
But where it meets another cell, the picture gives no clear answer.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.220
Both camps looked at similar preparations.

9
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.160
The light microscope cannot separate two points closer than about 0 .2 micrometers.

10
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.200
Two branches that touch fall inside that limit.

11
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.140
Branches that only pass close at different depths in a thick section look the same.

12
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.940
So the image cannot tell contact from fusion.

13
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:53.113
Each camp filled the blind spot with what its theory predicted.

14
00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:57.593
Golgi read the tangle of fine axon branches as a continuous net.

15
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:00.133
His network was made of axon branches.

16
00:01:00.973 --> 00:01:05.113
Cajal found axon branches that end close to other cells without joining them.

17
00:01:05.613 --> 00:01:07.993
For Cajal, those free endings were the evidence.

18
00:01:08.613 --> 00:01:12.613
Under the neuron theory, signals pass between cells at points of contact.

19
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:16.057
The direct test needed a finer instrument.

20
00:01:16.837 --> 00:01:21.197
In the 1950s, researchers used the electron microscope on synapses.

21
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:26.177
At a chemical synapse, the membranes of two cells stay separate across a narrow

22
00:01:26.177 --> 00:01:29.057
cleft. Small vesicles crowd in the terminal.

23
00:01:29.557 --> 00:01:31.917
The cleft is 10 to 20 nanometers wide.

24
00:01:32.377 --> 00:01:35.417
That is about 10 times smaller than the light microscope can resolve.

25
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.260
Electrical synapses add a qualification.

26
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.580
Gap junctions join some neurons, especially in development.

27
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.920
At an electrical synapse, channels cross from one cell's membrane to the next.

28
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.300
Ions and small molecules pass straight through.

29
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.900
Even so, each neuron keeps its own membrane.

30
00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.080
The channels pass only molecules up to roughly one kilodalton, and they can close.

31
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.860
So the neurons are coupled, but they are not one cell.

32
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:09.110
In vertebrates, Golgi's network, in the sense he meant it, does not exist.

33
00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:13.870
What exists is coupling through regulated pores between separate cells.

34
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:19.830
Keep this rule. Each vertebrate neuron keeps its own membrane, but a comb jelly's

35
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.230
nerve net shares one.
